 "result": "**Mechanism:** context-local proxies. Each of these names is a Werkzeug `LocalProxy` that reads a `contextvars.ContextVar` on every access. The result is per-thread and per-coroutine.\n\n**Where:** `src/tessel/globals.py`\n\n- `_app_var` (line 40) is the `ContextVar[AppContext]`. It holds the current `AppContext`.\n- `active_app` is `LocalProxy(_app_var, \"app\")`. It resolves to the current context's `.app`, the `Tessel` instance.\n- `g` is `LocalProxy(_app_var, \"g\")`. It resolves to the context's `.g`, an `_AppCtxGlobals` object.\n- `request` is `LocalProxy(_app_var, \"request\")`. It resolves to the context's `.request`, a `Request` object.\n- `app_ctx` (the context itself) and `session` use the same pattern.\n\n**Binding:** the `AppContext` class lives in `src/tessel/ctx.py` (imported under `TYPE_CHECKING` in `globals.py`). I didn't open that file. Entering the context, for example `with app.app_context()` or while handling a request, presumably sets `_app_var`, and leaving it resets the var.\n\n**Unbound case:** if the var isn't set, the proxy raises the \"Working outside of application context\" or \"outside of request context\" error. `request` and `session` use the request message. The others use the app message.\n\nRequest and app contexts have been merged into one `AppContext`. That is why `request_ctx` is deprecated and now returns `app_ctx`.",
